A||||4||Fierobruiser||Waxes and cleaners||||1||
Z||000000||Fierobruiser||11-22-2001||10:57 AM||||Has anyone tried Dura-shine? I've seen their infomercial and it sounds good and the principle of sheeting over beading seems logical enough. Although $19.95 ? plus shipping doesn't seem like a lot I'd rather not be the first guinea pig. My black 88 shows dirt and waterspots all the time. It would be great if when I came out in the morning the dew had washed my car clean. Also on Dream car Garage or one of the other shows, I caught the tail end of someone using something called Reflections? that was supposed to be great and easy on/off. Any test subjects out there for either of these products?<P>------------------<BR>Notchbacks Rule<BR>85GT(show car)<BR>88Coupe (daily driver)<BR>84 Coupe(project car)||||reg||1
Z||000001||avengador1||11-22-2001||11:32 AM||||I've tried Dura shine or shield or whatever it's called. It's only good if you have good paint to start with. It also doesn't last as long as a good carnuba wax paste and needs to be reapplied more often. It is easy on and off to apply, but nothing beats a real wax. If you wax your car corectly it will sheet the water off.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fierobruiser||11-22-2001||12:12 PM||||I've waxed cars for 39 years using everything from the best carnuba to Turtle Wax. They only sheet to a point and always leave water drops even when just waxed. On vertical surfaces like the doors everything sheets away. Hood, roof and deck always leave drops, except on the really steep slopes, that I have to chamois  or towel off. When I'm done it looks great but my question was whether the product actually sheets off all of the water from those horizontal surfaces as in their demo.<P>------------------<BR>Notchbacks Rule<BR>85GT(show car)<BR>88Coupe (daily driver)<BR>84 Coupe(project car)||||reg||1
Z||000003||avengador1||11-22-2001||05:06 PM||||It doesn't work like they advertise. It won't sheet off the entire car and you do get water beads. If you have a good wax base on your car it should be fairly well protected.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ka4nkf||11-22-2001||09:12 PM||||Trust me the best wax you can get is Simonize Vista paste wax. I started using this wax in the early 60's and for the last five years I have not been Abel<BR> to find any until the other day I was in sears and bought a buffer and guess what on the shelf was Simonize Vista I looked on the bottom of the can and it said distributed by Lock-Tight Corp. So they must have bought the patent from Simonize. Sears is the only place I have seen it. $5.99 per can.||||reg||1
